<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf of Riga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf of Riga is a large bay of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> between Latvia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, partly closed off from the open sea by the Estonian island of Saaremaa. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> and other rivers flow into it, and the ports of Riga and Pärnu stand on its shores. Its coasts were home to the Finnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a>, and from the 13th century it was the gateway for the German conquest and trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga.jpg" alt="Gulf of Riga" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: edited by M.Minderhoud · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locatie_Golf_van_Riga.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
